1
00:00:00,050 --> 00:00:03,792
Saturday morning, Malika sells flowers at a market stall.

2
00:00:03,842 --> 00:00:05,664
A buyer pays by card.

3
00:00:05,714 --> 00:00:09,504
The card reader on her table belongs to the bank, not to her.

4
00:00:09,554 --> 00:00:15,096
The buyer types a PIN into the bank's machine, and Malika only gets a receipt.

5
00:00:15,146 --> 00:00:21,216
In Lesson 4, card numbers went into one locked room, and ecom kept only card tokens.

6
00:00:21,266 --> 00:00:27,840
This lesson shows how the bank puts that same card reader on its web payment page, so ecom never sees the card.

7
00:00:27,890 --> 00:00:30,600
Most teams build the obvious design first.

8
00:00:30,650 --> 00:00:35,976
A form on ecom's page sends the card number to ecom's server, and ecom passes it on.

9
00:00:36,026 --> 00:00:39,072
That one hop puts every card number through ecom.

10
00:00:39,122 --> 00:00:42,912
Then ecom's whole system needs the full check from Lesson 4.

11
00:00:42,962 --> 00:00:45,408
So this system copies Malika's stall.

12
00:00:45,458 --> 00:00:47,280
Six steps make it work.

13
00:00:47,330 --> 00:00:51,240
First, ecom and the card zone never just talk freely.

14
00:00:51,290 --> 00:00:57,216
They agree on every question they may ask each other, and every answer, written down once.

15
00:00:57,266 --> 00:00:59,328
That agreement is a contract.

16
00:00:59,378 --> 00:01:05,616
ecom can only ask what the contract allows, and the card zone answers only in the shape the contract says.

17
00:01:05,666 --> 00:01:11,496
Second, when a buyer opens the payment page, ecom asks the card zone for a card entry session.

18
00:01:11,546 --> 00:01:16,488
A card entry session is a short lived place to type one card, for one payment.

19
00:01:16,538 --> 00:01:18,768
The card zone answers with an address.

20
00:01:18,818 --> 00:01:22,512
Third, the payment page shows that address inside a frame.

21
00:01:22,562 --> 00:01:28,152
The frame is a small window inside the page, and it belongs to the card zone, not to ecom.

22
00:01:28,202 --> 00:01:31,968
That frame is the bank's card reader, standing on ecom's counter.

23
00:01:32,018 --> 00:01:34,944
Fourth, the buyer types the card into the frame.

24
00:01:34,994 --> 00:01:38,328
The card goes straight from the buyer's browser to the card zone.

25
00:01:38,378 --> 00:01:40,272
ecom's servers are not on the way.

26
00:01:40,322 --> 00:01:47,952
Fifth, the card zone locks the number away and hands the page a receipt: the card token, the masked number, and the buyer's bank.

27
00:01:48,002 --> 00:01:49,824
Never the number itself.

28
00:01:49,874 --> 00:01:53,856
Sixth, ecom asks the card zone to charge the card by its token.

29
00:01:53,906 --> 00:01:58,992
Before it does, ecom checks that this token really came from this payment's own session.

30
00:01:59,042 --> 00:02:04,608
Sunday evening, a buyer orders roses on Malika's site and lands on the bank's payment page.

31
00:02:04,658 --> 00:02:07,488
At the top it says "Secure payment page of the bank".

32
00:02:07,538 --> 00:02:09,792
It shows the order and the amount to pay.

33
00:02:09,842 --> 00:02:14,208
Below sits a box called "Card details": the frame, the bank's reader.

34
00:02:14,258 --> 00:02:16,608
The buyer types the card number and the date.

35
00:02:16,658 --> 00:02:21,192
The box hands back one short line: the masked number and the buyer's bank.

36
00:02:21,242 --> 00:02:24,912
The buyer types the code sent to their phone and confirms.

37
00:02:24,962 --> 00:02:29,184
The page says "Taking the payment", then "Your bank is being asked".

38
00:02:29,234 --> 00:02:33,072
A few seconds later it says "Paid", with a reference number.

39
00:02:33,122 --> 00:02:36,312
Another buyer leaves the page open and goes to make tea.

40
00:02:36,362 --> 00:02:39,264
Or gets the card wrong too many times.

41
00:02:39,314 --> 00:02:45,096
The frame says the attempt ended, too many tries or the time ran out, and asks for the card again.

42
00:02:45,146 --> 00:02:47,784
The session was short lived on purpose.

43
00:02:47,834 --> 00:02:51,408
Each session binds one card only, and then it is used up.

44
00:02:51,458 --> 00:02:56,280
In the bank admin portal, Dilnoza sees the roses payment with a masked number.

45
00:02:56,330 --> 00:03:02,184
That is all ecom ever holds: the token, the mask, the buyer's bank and the card network.

46
00:03:02,234 --> 00:03:06,384
So ecom's servers, logs and backups never hold a card number.

47
00:03:06,434 --> 00:03:08,256
A leak there leaks no card.

48
00:03:08,306 --> 00:03:10,560
Malika never touches the card either.

49
00:03:10,610 --> 00:03:13,704
And the bank keeps its full check on one small room.

50
00:03:13,754 --> 00:03:15,576
But the token still matters.

51
00:03:15,626 --> 00:03:18,696
It can start a payment, so ecom guards it with care.

52
00:03:18,746 --> 00:03:21,432
That is why ecom keeps its smaller check.

53
00:03:21,482 --> 00:03:24,696
On Saturday, the reader sat on Malika's table.

54
00:03:24,746 --> 00:03:27,984
On the web, it sits inside ecom's page.

55
00:03:28,034 --> 00:03:31,512
Either way, the shop keeps receipts, never cards.

56
00:03:31,562 --> 00:03:34,536
Next: how every service gets its settings.
